  "query_preview": "..Heat will continue to be the main story this week as\na strong area of high pressure remains over the Southwest. This\nsynoptic pattern has brought hot daytime temperatures (roughly \n5-15 degrees above normal), and limited chances for\nshowers/storms. Although the available moisture is not abundant\nthroughout our CWA, there's still enough to see some diurnally\ndriven activity each afternoon across the higher terrain. Activity\nis forecast to be mostly focused across the eastern Rim and White\nMountains today and Tuesday, before coverage expands westward by\nmid-week. The westward expansion of showers/storms will be due to\nthe strong ridge wobbling northward, but there is still some\nuncertainty with where the high will eventually settle.\nRegardless, we can anticipate more showers/storms to break out\nacross the higher terrain by Wednesday. \n\nWith slightly cooler temperatures coming in later this week with\nthe movement of the high/increased moisture, we do not expect to\nhave any heat headlines Wednesday onward at this time. The one \nlocation that could still be up for question is the lower \nelevations of the Grand Canyon. For now, an Extreme Heat Warning \nremains in place for the lower elevations of Yavapai County, the \nlower elevations of the Grand Canyon, and Marble and Glen Canyons\ntoday, with Heat Advisories in place for many locations across NE\nArizona. The Heat Warning for the Grand Canyon and Marble and Glen\nCanyons extends through Tuesday evening as well. \n\nIn short, expect hot daytime temperatures to persist and daily\nchances for showers/storms primarily across the higher terrain.",
